---
id: org.dxos.plugin.cloudflare
name: CloudflarePlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

A **headless** Composer plugin that connects a Cloudflare account. It has no
React surfaces and syncs nothing into ECHO. It contributes one thing: a
**Connector** entry (`source: "cloudflare.com"`) that runs Cloudflare's OAuth
flow through `kms-service` and leaves the grant behind as an `AccessToken`
plus a `Connection`.

Everything downstream — an operation, an assistant tool, another plugin —
resolves that token through `CredentialsService` keyed on the source, the same
seam `plugin-ideogram` uses for its API key. Nothing here knows what the token
will be used for.

The grant is sized for that downstream work rather than for this plugin: a
coding agent that names the resources it needs should be able to deploy a
Worker and bind its storage without the user touching the dashboard.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                     |
| ------ | ----------------------- |
| `feat` | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0` |
| `test` | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0` |
| `flow` | `org.dxos.mdl.flow@1.0` |

## Connector

```mdl
feat CloudflareConnector
  desc: >
    Contributes a ConnectorEntry via Capability.contribute(Connector, [entry])
    on the ConnectorEvents.Start activation event. OAuth only — no sync, no
    credential form.
  id: cloudflare
  source: cloudflare.com
  label: Cloudflare
  oauth:
    provider: OAuthProvider.CLOUDFLARE
    scopes: CLOUDFLARE_OAUTH_SCOPES
    # Identity plus the deploy and resource-management set: Workers scripts,
    # bindings, routes, tail and observability; KV, R2, D1, Queues, Pipelines,
    # Vectorize, Hyperdrive and Secrets Store; Workers AI; Containers; zone
    # read and certificates. Scope ids come from
    # Cloudflare's GET /oauth/scopes, NOT from wrangler's colon-delimited
    # namespace, which a self-managed client rejects. offline_access is absent:
    # Cloudflare attaches it from the client's refresh-token grant and rejects
    # the authorization request when it is named. Register every product scope
    # past the identity reads as optional; a declined scope is warned about by
    # kms-service and the flow continues.
  onTokenCreated: >
    GET /user for the email (falling back to username, then to the first
    account name from GET /accounts) and write it to accessToken.account.
    Wrapped in Effect.orDie so a failed lookup cannot discard the Connection.
  testConnection: >
    GET /accounts with the stored token. Non-2xx or transport failure maps to
    ConnectionTestError, which lets the connection UI offer reauthentication.
```

## Dependencies

- `@dxos/plugin-connector` (workspace:\*) — `ConnectorSpec.Connector` capability
  key and `ConnectionTestError`.
- `@dxos/compute` — `Credential.getApiKeyValue` for the stored token.
- `@dxos/protocols` — `OAuthProvider.CLOUDFLARE`, whose provider spec lives in
  `kms-service`.
- `@dxos/app-framework`, `@dxos/app-toolkit`, `@dxos/echo`, `@dxos/link`,
  `effect`.
- No `react` / `@dxos/react-ui*`.

## Acceptance

```mdl
test T-1: Label from the user endpoint
  given: a connector token whose grant carries user-details.read
  when: onTokenCreated runs against a /user response with an email
  then: accessToken.account is that email
```

```mdl
test T-2: Label falls back to the account name
  given: a grant carrying memberships.read but not user-details.read
  when: onTokenCreated runs and /user answers 403
  then: accessToken.account is the first account's name from /accounts
```

```mdl
test T-3: A rejected token fails the connection test
  given: a revoked grant
  when: testConnection runs and /accounts answers 401
  then: it fails with ConnectionTestError
```

## Out of scope (v1)

- Syncing any Cloudflare resource (Workers, zones, R2, D1) into ECHO. The
  connector declares no `sync`, so `plugin-connector` offers no bindings for it.
- Operations or assistant tools over the v4 API. This plugin holds the
  credential; calling the API is somebody else's job.
- Cloudflare API tokens as an alternative to OAuth (no `credentialForm`).
- Narrowing the grant per use. Every scope is requested at connect time; there
  is no path for an agent to ask for `d1:write` only when it first needs D1.

## QA

```mdl
flow QA-1: Connect a Cloudflare account
  desc: |
    Headless — the whole plugin is one Connector entry, so the flow is the
    generic connector UI end to end. There is no plugin-local operation to
    invoke.
  actors: both
  covers: [CloudflareConnector, T-1, T-3]
  status: passing            # 2026-09-09; run by a human against EDGE dev, steps 1-3
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - a space is open
    - a Cloudflare account the user can sign in to
    - no Cloudflare connection already exists
  test:
    - name: Start the OAuth flow
      do: Open Settings -> Connectors -> Cloudflare and click Connect.
      expect: a popup opens on dash.cloudflare.com asking to authorize the listed scopes
      note: NO `invoke` — the flow is driven by the connector coordinator, not an operation.
    - name: Authorize
      do: Sign in if prompted and approve the request.
      expect: |
        the popup closes and the connection appears with the account's email as
        its label; an AccessToken (source "cloudflare.com") and a Connection
        referencing it now exist
    - name: Test the connection
      do: Open the new connection.
      expect: the connection tests clean and offers no reauthentication prompt
  after:
    - name: Disconnect
      do: In Settings -> Connectors -> Cloudflare, remove the connection.
      expect: the Connection and its AccessToken are removed
```
